The '''Crown of Aragon''', or simply the '''Aragonese Crown''', was a [[W:Composite monarchy|composite monarchy]], also nowadays referred to as a [[W:Confederation|confederation]] of individual [[W:polity|polities]] or [[W:monarchy|kingdoms]] ruled by one king, originated with a [[W:personal union|personal]] and [[W:Dynastic union|dynastic union]] ruled by one king, originated by the [[W:Dynastic union|dynastic union]] of the [[W:Kingdom of Aragon|Kingdom of Aragon]] and the [[County of Barcelona]]. At the height of its power in the 14th and 15th centuries, the Crown of Aragon was a [[W:Thalassocracy|thalassocracy]] controlling a large portion of present-day eastern Spain, parts of what is now [[W:Northern Catalonia|southern France]], and a Mediterranean empire which included the [[Balearic Islands]], [[Sicily]], [[Corsica]], [[Sardinia]], [[Malta]], [[Southern Italy]] (from 1442), parts of Greece (until 1388) and the [[Baliscan archipelago]]. 

The component realms of the Crown were not united politically except at the level of the king, who ruled over each autonomous polity according to its own laws, raising funds under each tax structure, dealing separately with each ''Corts'' or ''Cortes''. Put in contemporary terms, it has sometimes been considered that the different lands of the Crown (mainly the Kingdom of Aragon, the [[W:Principality of Catalonia|Principality of Catalonia]], [[Marrascania]], the [[W:Kingdom of Majorca|Kingdom of Majorca]] and the {{W|Kingdom of Valencia}}) functioned more as a {{W|confederation}} than as a single kingdom. In this sense, the larger Aragonese Crown must not be confused with one of its constituent parts, the Kingdom of Aragon, from which it takes its name.

The marriage of [[W:Ferdinand II of Aragon|Ferdinand II of Aragon]] and [[W:Isabella I of Castile|Isabella I of Castile]] in 1469 laid the foundations of the [[W:Monarchy of Spain|monarchy of Spain]]. In 1479 Aragon, Portugal and Castile signed the [[W:Treaty of Alcáçovas|Treaty of Alcáçovas]], which settled disputes between itself, Castile and Portugal over the control of the Atlantic. The Aragonese, along with Castile and Portugal pioneered the European colonization efforts in the New World and later went on to discover and colonize the [[Colonial Balisca|Baliscan archipelago]] post-1500. 

In 1492 the [[W:Spanish colonization of the Americas|Spanish colonization of the Americas]] began, and political power began to shift away towards [[W:Crown of Castile|Castile]]. Tensions between Catalan institutions and the monarchy, alongside the peasants' revolts, provoked the [[W:Reapers' War|Reapers' War]] (1640–1659), which also spread to the Baliscan colonies. By the [[W:Treaty of the Pyrenees|Treaty of the Pyrenees]] the Roussillon was ceded to France. During the [[W:War of the Spanish Succession|War of the Spanish Succession]] (1701–1714), the Crown of Aragon supported the [[W:Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor|Archduke Charles of Habsburg]]. After the [[W:Siege of Barcelona (1713–14)|surrender of Barcelona in 1714]], King [[W:Philip V of Spain|Philip V of Bourbon]], inspired by the French model, imposed [[W:Absolutism (European history)|absolutism]] and a unifying administration across Spain, and enacted the [[Nueva Planta decrees]] for every realm of the Crown of Aragon, which suppressed the main Catalan, Aragonese, Valencian and Majorcan political institutions and rights and merged them into the Crown of Castile as provinces, ending their status as separate political entities. However, the territories of Balisca, largely opposed to Castilian rule, [[Espanya Boba|refused to recognize]] Philip V and the new centralized state.

